= Sensory processing — Simple =

'''Sensory processing''' is how nervous systems detect and transform information from the body and environment.

Sensory receptors respond to things such as:

* light;
* sound;
* touch;
* body position;
* temperature;
* chemicals;
* pain-related stimuli.

The nervous system changes these signals as they travel through neural pathways. Some information is combined, some is suppressed, and some is emphasized.

== Transduction ==

'''Transduction''' is the first conversion step.

A sensory receptor changes physical or chemical stimulation into a biological electrical or chemical response.

Examples:

* photoreceptors respond to light;
* mechanoreceptors respond to pressure or movement;
* hair cells in the inner ear respond to mechanical vibration;
* chemoreceptors respond to molecules.

The neural signal is not a perfect copy of the original stimulus. It is a representation created by the receptor and neural pathway.<ref name="purves">[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10955/ Purves et al., ''Neuroscience'', Sensation and Sensory Processing.]</ref>

== Different senses use different receptors ==

Vision, hearing, touch, smell, taste, balance, and body position use different receptor systems.

The brain can tell sensory modalities apart partly because different pathways connect to different neural circuits.

This is sometimes called the '''labeled-line''' principle.

== Receptive fields ==

A receptive field is the region where a stimulus can affect a sensory neuron.

For touch, it may be an area of skin.

For vision, it may be part of the visual field.

Small receptive fields can support more precise location judgments.

Fingertips have much finer spatial resolution than many areas of the back.<ref name="receptors">[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539861/ “Physiology, Sensory Receptors.”]</ref>

== Sensory maps ==

Many sensory systems preserve organized maps.

Examples include:

* visual space mapped in visual cortex;
* body regions mapped in somatosensory cortex;
* sound frequencies mapped in auditory pathways.

These are neural maps, not tiny pictures or sounds stored in the brain.

== Intensity and timing ==

The nervous system can represent stimulus intensity using:

* firing rate;
* number of active neurons;
* timing patterns;
* population activity.

Timing can also carry important information.

The auditory system uses extremely small timing differences between the ears to help locate sounds.

== Adaptation ==

Sensory systems change their sensitivity when stimulation remains constant.

After entering a dark room, vision gradually becomes more sensitive.

A constant smell may become less noticeable.

Adaptation helps sensory systems use their limited response range efficiently.

It is more than simple fatigue; neural circuits actively adjust to recent stimulus statistics.<ref name="adaptive">[https://doi.org/10.1038/s41593-021-00846-0 Młynarski and Hermundstad (2021), “Efficient and adaptive sensory codes.”]</ref>

== Perception is not the same as sensation ==

[[Perception]] is the interpretation and organization of sensory information.

Sensory processing begins before conscious awareness.

So:

<pre>
receptor activity
does not automatically mean
conscious perception
</pre>

Scientists can measure neural responses to stimuli that a person does not consciously report.

== Attention changes processing ==

[[Attention]] can increase the processing of information that is important for a task.

For example, listening for one voice in a noisy room can change how auditory information is processed.

Attention affects processing; it does not create the physical stimulus.

== Several senses can work together ==

Natural perception is often multisensory.

Seeing a speaker's face can help understand speech.

Vision and balance interact during movement.

Touch and vision can combine during object handling.

The nervous system combines these signals in several brain regions rather than one single multisensory center.<ref name="multi">[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK92848/ Murray and Wallace, eds., ''The Neural Bases of Multisensory Processes''.]</ref>

== Measuring sensory ability ==

Psychophysics measures how physical stimuli affect behavior or reports.

Researchers can test:

* the faintest stimulus someone detects;
* the smallest difference they can notice;
* how perceived intensity changes;
* reaction time.

A detection threshold is probabilistic rather than a perfectly sharp boundary.

== Signal detection ==

A person can miss a real stimulus or report one when none was present.

Signal detection theory separates:

* '''sensitivity''' — ability to distinguish signal from noise;
* '''response bias''' — willingness to say the signal is present.

This prevents cautious or liberal response styles from being mistaken for changes in sensory ability.

== Learning and sensory plasticity ==

Sensory systems can change with experience.

Practice can improve discrimination.

Development, injury, and altered sensory experience can reorganize neural responses.

Sensory processing is therefore adaptable.

== Behavior and sensory processing ==

[[Behavior]] can reveal what an organism detects.

But behavior depends on more than sensation.

Motivation, attention, learning, and decision rules affect whether a detected stimulus produces a response.

== Machines also process sensor data ==

Robots and electronic devices can transform camera, microphone, temperature, or motion signals.

This is a useful engineering analogy.

It does not mean a machine experiences sensations consciously.

